  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting Children&apos;&apos;s School Readiness and Retention (Uganda)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Although the conflict in Northern Uganda conflict has ended and most people have returned from refugee camps to their communities, education provision is poor. Whilst the number of children going to primary school in northern Uganda has increased, many children are failing to learn and are dropping out of school. For children in remote rural areas, school is often far away, the quality of education is poor and there is limited support from parents at home, many of whom missed out on an education themselves because of many years of conflict. This project aims to break this cycle of low achievement and retention. It combines work in local communities, work in schools, and influencing government policy to achieve change. 800 new low cost home learning centres will be set up in villages in eight districts of northern Uganda. Each centre will be linked to a primary school. The centres will provide informal early childhood care and education for children under 6. At weekends and in the evenings (lit by solar lamps) children who are at school can study at the centres. Classes will be run in the community for adults in literacy and numeracy. Village health teams will visit the centres to ensure that children are healthy and well-nourished. The project will work with government to improve the quality of teaching in school, especially by promoting &#x2018;mother tongue&apos; education in the first three years of primary school and to strengthen home-school links. At a national level, the project will aim to influence implementation of government policy on early childhood and early primary education. As a result of the project, significantly more children from poor rural communities in northern Uganda will enrol in, stay in and achieve better in school.</narrative>
  </description>
